  "worked_solution": "First, we find the polar coordinates of the point $A.$ The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{(-3\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+27} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{(-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|-\\sqrt{3}| \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are $(r,\\theta )=(6,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}).$\n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =k\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 6 & =k\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) \\\\ 6 & =k(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ k & =-4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $r=-4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253930/assets/69d814c075c7e8a940fe4900f5601052835ab999e68e43fabe1dd34eaf5709b2.png)",
